  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1213 PM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nDry southwest flow continues across the region today under somewhat \nflattened ridging aloft. Low pressure moves in tonight and slowly \nadvances eastward through Friday. Behind this low, caa takes hold. \nAhead of the low, breezy conditions will continue to draw up warmer \nair especially on the downslope areas of the Coteau. Most models \nstill show best moisture convergence in MN by 21z Friday. In spite \nof SPC's slight risk that stretches back across the Coteau, none of \nthe hi-res are overly impressed with storm chances in SD. However, \nif something does form in the mildly capped (+11C), dry environment, \nstrong winds would be a threat.\n\nThe other concern with this low and cold front will be those 30 to \n40 mph wind gusts over dry fuels in central SD. Min RH south of \nPierre is forecast to fall to 20 to 25 percent. While not strictly \nred flag criteria, the strength of the winds could necessitate a \nheadline. Very high grassland fire danger is expected, either way. \n\nOn Saturday, high pressure briefly builds in with H85 temps falling \nto around +10C to +12C. Temperatures will be markedly cooler, even a \nfew degrees below seasonal averages, with highs in the upper 60 to \nlower 70s. The cooler, more fall-like temps will persist through \nearly next week. \n\nSunday into Monday, low pressure moves off the Rockies into the \nPlains. Shortwave energy will eject out over the northern plains \nahead of an upper low in Montana. All of this will result in some \nwidespread rainfall. Given this is expected to fall rather slowly \nand some models have backed off on overall QPF amounts, the precip \nshould be welcome.",
